00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.850
Let's look at agentic inference
from a storage-centric view.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.270
Any agent that leverages
enterprise data is built

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.230
on a data pipeline anchored
in object storage,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.050
or Azure Blob, with two stages.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:34.020
Stage 1: Bringing
your enterprise data

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.960
to AI services and agents.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.640
This is the data preparation
pipeline, ingestion, cleaning,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.720
chunking, embedding, and
indexing into a vector database

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.750
so that the agent can
do grounded retrieval

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.580
with the right context.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.150
Intermediate representations,
namely the embeddings, indexes,

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.820
process chunks get
stored on Azure Blob

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.850
so they can be retrieved
with milliseconds latency

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:03.990
at query time without
rerunning the entire pipeline.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:09.900
Stage 2: Retrieve, store, and
manage AI data at runtime.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.100
Every turn of the agent loop
multiplies load on storage,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.380
more retrievals from blob
and the vector index,

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.400
more test time compute, and an
exponential explosion of logs,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.880
conversation state, tool
outputs, and agent traces

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.950
that all have to be persisted
for fast retrieval downstream.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.670
At scale, distributed
inference also swaps models

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.700
in and out across GPUs.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.000
That gives us three critical
storage requirements

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.750
for agentic inference:
one, making it easy

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.600
to bring enterprise
data securely to agents

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.770
and converting it into
agent-accessible knowledge; two,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.730
distributing and loading models
onto AI compute fast enough

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:57.150
to keep expensive
GPUs busy; and three,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.360
optimizing infrastructure
utilization to drive

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.840
up tokens per watt per dollar.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.100
The rest of this talk maps
directly to those three.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.280
Let's start with optimizing
inference itself.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.340
And the biggest lever we
have there right now is

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.900
prompt caching.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.600
Now that we've set the context
for top storage requirements

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.420
for agentic inference,
let's go one layer deep

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.940
into where the next big
wave of inference cost

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.030
and performance pressure is
showing up, prompt caching.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.400
As enterprises move
from single-turn chat

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.280
to long multi-turn
agentic workflows,

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.970
the way prompt tokens flow
through the GPUs is becoming one

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:40.590
of the largest drivers of
inference cost and latency.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.960
My colleague Vishnu and
I are going to walk you

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.330
through what's happening and
what we are doing about it

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:49.140
at Microsoft to solve for the
problem of prompt caching.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:50.880
To set the stage, let's look

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.690
at how inference works
today without caching.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.530
Modern AI agents run on long,
static enterprise context,

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.220
system instructions, tool
definitions, retrieval context.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.410
And they invoke these
prompt contexts

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:07.080
across millions of invocations.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.350
In most production agents,
the static, unchanged portion

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:15.090
of the prompt represents more
than 60% of inference cost.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.290
Here is why.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.610
On every turn, the GPU has to
recompute the model attention

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.940
over the entire prefix,
the static system prompt,

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.030
the tool definitions, and
all of those prior turns.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:30.750
Turn two adds even more
history on top of that.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.930
And then turn three
adds even more.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.000
By the time you're
a few turns in,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.550
the GPU is burning
expensive cycles,

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.420
re-dealing the same tokens
it has already seen before.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:46.080
That recompute is what drives
up Time to First Token,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.340
or as we call it, TTFT, and the
end-to-end inference latency,

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:53.490
and further, what makes
long context agents

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.470
so expensive to operate.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.380
Now let's apply prompt
caching to the same picture.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.750
The idea is simple
but very powerful.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.450
Instead of recomputing
attention on every turn,

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.750
we cache the KV state of
the repeated context token

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.030
and reuse it for the next turn.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.120
On turn 1, the GPU still
processes the full prefix once,

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.120
but as it does it, it also
writes the attention state

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.910
out as a context-cached object.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.940
Turn 2, the GPU doesn't
redo that work.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.570
It just loads the cache state
and only computes the attention

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.700
on the new tokens for that turn.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.960
Turn 3 and every turn after
that looks exactly the same.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.180
The result is a
dramatic reduction

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:34.910
in GPU compute per turn.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:39.320
And customers can get inference
cost savings up to 90% along

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:42.350
with significantly lower
time to first token.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.230
In fact, this is so impactful
that model providers

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:49.560
like OpenAI already offer
implicit prompt caching APIs

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:50.970
out of the box.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.530
That's a great starting point.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.260
But for enterprise scenarios,
our customers are building

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.500
by using open source models
or their own models,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.040
that alone is not enough.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.460
And that's exactly what Vishnu
and I will talk about next.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.210
VISHNU CHARAN:
Thank you, Saurabh.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.010
Hi. I'm Vishnu Charan,
a Product Manager

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.980
in the Azure Storage team
focused on AI workloads.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.720
Picking up on explicit caching,

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.780
the reason that tier works
is the cache no longer lives

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:16.880
on the GPU node.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.430
It lives on durable
Azure storage.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.920
And once it's on Azure storage,
you get the full scale

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.040
of Azure Blob behind your
KVCache, effectively,

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.230
unlimited capacity,
configurable retention,

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:28.860
and multi-region reach.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.320
That same idea is also how
we are meeting the open

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.730
source community.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:37.080
NIXL is NVIDIA's inference
transfer library, a unified API

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.560
for moving model weights and
KV blocks across memory tiers

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.650
with a pluggable
backend underneath.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.530
You can see those backends along
the bottom of the diagram.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.320
We've contributed a
first-class Azure Blob backend

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.400
into that role.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.390
So any framework that already
speaks NIXL, like vLLM, Dynamo,

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.870
TensorRT-LLM, or Ray, can
now read and write KV blocks

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:01.170
to Azure Blob with zero
custom adapter code.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:03.570
The most immediate
payoff is in LMCache,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:08.100
the open source KVCache
layer widely used with vLLM.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.130
Through our plugin, LMCache
offloads KV blocks straight

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.370
to Blob so the picture is
consistent across the stack.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:17.040
Managed customers land
on Azure Context Cache.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.580
OSS developers land on Azure
Blob via NIXL and LMCache.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.820
Both parts put KVCache
on durable Azure storage

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.730
that travels with the
workload, not the node.

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.500
And with that, let me
show you what this looks

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:29.880
like in practice.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.200
Here's what we've got running.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.670
An inference app on vLLM
serving LAMA 3.3, 70 billion,

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.820
Tensor parallel across
all four GPUs on this VM,

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.940
loading the model from a
blob storage container.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:45.420
LMCache sits alongside vLLM,
building the KV cache.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.880
Now to drive it, I'm using
a simple Python client

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:52.020
that posts an OpenAI-compatible
request to that vLLM endpoint

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:53.310
with a fairly punishing prompt,

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:56.430
roughly 100,000
tokens of context.

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.280
And there it is, 52 seconds
for the first token.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.160
That's the GPUs doing
pre-fill from scratch,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.520
the long context cold start
everyone is familiar with.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.350
Now let's run NIXL again.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.340
Here's the config.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:11.370
Chunk size 256,
local CPU tier off,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.610
because I want NIXL doing the
work, not a CPU cache layer.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:17.760
And here's the new
piece, NIXL storage on,

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.470
backend set to Azure Blob,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.080
async put so rights
never block inference,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.560
plus the storage account and
container we're targeting.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:28.530
Now I restart vLLM with the
LMCache connector enabled

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.960
and fire the same prompt.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.810
As tokens stream out, NIXL
writes the KV chunks straight

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.850
to blob in the background.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:40.020
We pay GPU prefill once, but
the cache is being populated.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.480
Quick look in the portal,
and there they are.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.960
Around 1,500 chunks,
about 20 megabytes each,

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.370
sitting durably
in the container.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.400
The full KV state persistent.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.140
Now for the real test.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.230
I restart vLLM, wiping
GPU and CPU memory

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.150
for a genuine cold start.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:58.380
Same prompt.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.170
This time, NIXL streams
those KV chunks directly

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.050
from Blob into GPU memory.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:04.890
Watch the network.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:05.730
Here's EndLoad.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.180
We're pretty close to the
line rate on this 80-gig NIC.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:10.530
The bottleneck just moved

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.960
from GPU compute to
network bandwidth.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.700
Exactly the trade we want.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.550
And the time to first
token, about 8 seconds.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.010
Just by streaming
the KV out of Blob,

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.220
the model loads roughly
6 times faster.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:27.210
That's the NIXL Blob plugin,
turning a 100k token cold start

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:31.110
from a 52-second wait
into an 8-second stream.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.200
The NIXL demo you just saw was
the inference optimization

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.330
story: KVCache
streamed out of Blob

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:39.330
so cold start don't
pay pre-fill twice.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:42.510
But the KVCache is only half
of the cold start story.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.100
Every cold start also has

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.720
to load the model
weights themselves

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.090
from storage onto the GPU.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.070
For small models,
that's seconds.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.590
For the really large
ones, it's minutes.

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:54.660
Now, I want to
focus on that half,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:57.000
fast model loading
and distribution.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.950
Same problem, same fix.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.710
Saturate the NIC
straight out of blobs.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:04.260
The Run AI model streamer
is an open-source loader

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:05.970
for large model weights.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.520
It plugs straight into the
engines people actually run

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:11.160
in production: vLLM and SGLang.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.470
Drop it in, point it
at a storage account,

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.020
and it replaces the
default loader.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.990
Cold starts are the tax
every GPU pays when it comes

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:23.010
to order scaling out, recovering
a failed node, rotating a model,

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:25.650
or loading a fine
tune on demand.

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.380
With the default loader,
that tax is unpredictable

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.450
and minutes long
on the big models.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:34.050
So the bigger your fleet, the
more capacity you're burning,

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.060
just waiting for
weights to land.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.280
Here's what Streamer
does differently.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.470
It splits the model
file into chunks,

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.660
pulls them from object
storage in parallel,

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.810
stages them in CPU memory,
and pushes those chunks

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:48.060
to the GPU memory concurrently.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.960
The reads and copies overlap,
so the NIC stays saturated

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.230
and the GPU is fed as fast
as the network allows.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.960
And here's the piece we
built, the Azure Blob plugin

00:14:57.960 --> 00:14:59.880
for the running AI Streamer.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:04.170
Weights flow straight out of
Blob through CPU into the GPU.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.300
The Blob container is
the source of truth,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.000
and the NIC is the
only bottleneck.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.640
And we get three to six
times faster cold starts

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.220
than the default
loader consistently.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.690
Let me show you it running.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:19.260
Same VM as before, ND96, H100.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.360
It has eight H100s on board
with an 80-gig NIC on the back.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:27.570
The model I'm loading is
gpt-oss-120b, about 60 gigs

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:28.830
of weight, pre-staged

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:32.010
as safetensor shards
in Blob container.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.830
Same storage account, same
plumbing as the NIXL demo,

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:36.960
just a different
artifact this time.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:41.610
Now I start vLLM, standard vLLM
serve command with one switch.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:44.610
Load format set to
"runai_ streamer."

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.260
That's the entire integration.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.350
Point it at the blob container,
and the streamer takes over.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.740
Chunked parallel reads
from blobs, CPU staging,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.160
concurrent CPU, GPU
copies, all overlapping.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:57.270
Now watch the NIC.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:00.870
About 86 gigabits a
second on an 80-gig link.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:03.420
The NIC is fully saturated.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:05.580
The streamer is pulling
everything Blob will give it,

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:08.280
and the GPUs are eating
it as fast as it lands.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.490
This is what loading and
network speed looks like.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:14.010
And there it is,
all 8 GPUs loaded.

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:15.570
Application startup complete.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:18.630
Sixty gigs of
weights into 8 H100s

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.120
in roughly the time
it took to say this.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.760
One more cut at model loading.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.730
This time at AK scale.

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:27.660
Everything I have shown

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:30.810
so far has been a single
pod, a single GPU box.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:32.340
But production isn't one pod.

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:35.010
It's 100, and every one of
them has to pull the model

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:37.920
down before it can
serve a single token.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:41.220
If you run the numbers,
a 70b model is 140 gigs.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:45.420
A hundred pods coming up
together is 14 terabytes coming

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:47.070
out of Blob all at once.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.770
You hit throttling,
the NIC saturates,

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.720
and cold start
stretch 10 minutes.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.300
Every one of those minutes
is GPU you're paying for

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:55.740
and getting nothing back.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.670
So we built a distributed cache
inside the AK cluster itself.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:01.890
Zero changes to the
workflow on top.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:05.370
Hugging Face Loader, BlobFuse,
the Run AI Streamer,

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:07.950
they all sit on top
of it unchanged.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:12.030
Underneath, every GPU node is
both a cache and a client.

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:15.510
Local NVMe RAID holds the hard
data served at NVMe speed

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:18.000
with zero copy send file.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.030
Anything missing comes in
over the network from a peer

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:24.390
with increased acceleration
on the fan inside.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:27.630
First pod warms the
cache, the next 99 read

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:29.250
from inside the cluster, two

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:32.460
and a half times faster
model loading on AKS.

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:33.150
Let me show it.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.270
Here's the live comparison.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:39.470
Twelve gigs of LLM weights
loaded on a real cluster.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.120
Direct from Blob,

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:44.280
1,500 megabytes a second,
8 seconds to load.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.400
With the distributed cache,
4,000 megabytes a second,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.980
3 seconds, 2.5 times faster.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.720
And when 100 pods
come up together,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:53.790
they all read from the cache.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.660
Blob gets hit exactly once.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:18:00.200
That's Azure Container Storage's
new distributed cache,

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.870
built for AI on AKS.

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:05.450
Distributed cache is in
early access right now.

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:09.440
NVMe class latency, throughput
to local network saturation,

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:13.040
Kubernetes native, and minimum
to zero changes on your site.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:16.610
If you're running AI on AKS,
this is the one to try.

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.860
Scan the QR, sign up
for the early test,

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:20.390
and we'll get you on it.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:23.760
That's the inference half
of the story on compute.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:27.300
KVCache going out to Blob,
weights coming back in,

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:30.900
both at line rate, but none
of that runs without data.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:34.380
The model itself, the
cache, the context windows,

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:37.650
the retrieved documents,
all of it lives in storage.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:38.490
And for inference,

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:41.030
the bottleneck isn't
whether you have the data.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:44.630
It's how fast you can
feed it into the GPU.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.210
So the question for this
last section is simple.

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.240
How do you get your data
sitting in Blob in front

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:52.940
of an inference engine
as fast as possible?

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.040
That's our third pillar,
bringing enterprise data

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:00.300
to AI inference and converting
it to agent-ready knowledge.

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:01.890
So how do you actually
get to that data

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.930
from inside your AI stack?

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:05.310
That's what this slide is about.

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:09.030
And this revolves around one
key idea: We meet developers

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.860
where they are with
native integrations

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:13.800
across the entire AI ecosystem.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:16.800
If you look at that list
there, BlobFuse and AzCopy

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.130
on the data movement side,
ADLFS, the PyTorch connector,

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.430
the LangChain data loader
on the framework side,

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.500
plus the two ecosystem
integrations you just saw

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:29.220
demoed, Run AI Model
Streamer and NVIDIA NIXL.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:33.270
Effectively, whatever stack
you're on, Python, Spark, Ray,

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:34.980
PyTorch, or an agent framework,

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:36.390
you get to Blob
without friction.

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.600
A few tools worth
calling out here.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.030
BlobFuse gives you
a POSIX-like mount,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:45.270
so you can treat object storage
like a local file system.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.560
Same experience across
VMs, containers, and AKs.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:53.850
ADLFS is the Python-native
fsspec-compatible interface,

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:55.770
which makes Blob
instantly usable

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.170
from Dask, Pandas, and RAID.

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:00.810
For deep learning, the PyTorch
connector plugs directly

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:03.600
into Dataset, DataLoader,
and Checkpoint APIs.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:08.040
No custom code, zero config
auth with Azure Identity.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.110
Then, the performance-critical
parts for agentic

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:11.700
and inference workloads.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.340
We saw Run:AI Model Streamer
cutting cold start times,

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.250
loading models from
Blob 6x faster.

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:19.890
NIXL drops us straight into
NVIDIA's data movement stack

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:22.920
with KVCache offload and
high-performance model transfers

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:26.010
across Dynamo, vLLM,
Ray, and the rest.

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:29.280
And the last one, the
LangChain Azure Blob loader.

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.740
Faster ingestion and
lower memory footprint

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:34.320
when you're working with
large document (inaudible).

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:36.390
Five to nine times
faster lazy-load,

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:39.270
seven to ten times
faster alazy-load

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.980
against the community loader.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.370
So Blob isn't just scalable
infrastructure underneath.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:47.550
It's deeply integrated into
the AI developer ecosystem

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:52.410
with high performance, secure,
seamless access across training,

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:54.510
inference, and agent workflows.

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:56.580
And that's the OSS layer.

